begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-005','[["Distinguishing risks from issues","A supplier delivery has already missed its due date, but the team continues to record it only as a future risk.","Manage the missed delivery as an issue while updating any related future risks and response plans.","Keep it as a risk because all negative events are risks.","Delete it from all records because the event has already occurred.","Wait for project closure before deciding whether it was an issue.","A risk is uncertain and may occur; an issue has occurred and requires current resolution, ownership and escalation as appropriate."],["Risk management planning","Team members use different probability scales, escalation thresholds and reporting formats, producing inconsistent risk decisions.","Define the risk management approach, roles, categories, scales, thresholds, review frequency and reporting rules.","Allow each workstream to invent its own scales without coordination.","Assess risks only when the sponsor asks for a report.","Use financial impact as the only risk criterion.","Risk management planning establishes a consistent and proportionate framework for identifying, analysing, responding to and monitoring risk."],["Risk identification","The project manager prepared the risk register alone and omitted technical, commercial, community and regulatory uncertainties.","Use a cross-functional identification process involving relevant stakeholders, historical information, assumptions and structured techniques.","Keep the register private so stakeholders do not become concerned.","Record only risks that the project manager can personally control.","Limit identification to events that occurred on the previous project.","Broad participation and multiple information sources improve the completeness and quality of risk identification."],["Risk categorisation and Risk Breakdown Structure","The risk register contains a long undifferentiated list, making patterns and concentration of exposure difficult to see.","Categorise risks using a suitable Risk Breakdown Structure and analyse clusters and root sources.","Sort risks only by the alphabetical order of their titles.","Create a separate register for every individual risk.","Remove categories because they may reveal systemic weaknesses.","Categorisation supports completeness checks, ownership, aggregation and understanding of systemic exposure."],["Qualitative risk prioritisation","The team allocates equal attention to every risk although probability, impact, urgency and proximity differ significantly.","Apply agreed qualitative criteria to prioritise risks and focus management effort on the most significant exposures.","Prioritise only the risks raised by senior stakeholders.","Rank risks by the length of their descriptions.","Treat low-probability catastrophic risks as automatically negligible.","Qualitative analysis uses defined criteria such as probability, impact, urgency and detectability to support prioritisation."],["Risk matrix limitations","Two risks receive the same red rating, but one could cause a minor delay while the other could threaten public safety.","Examine the underlying impact dimensions and escalation thresholds rather than relying only on the colour rating.","Treat all red risks as identical in consequence and response.","Downgrade the safety risk so the register has fewer red items.","Use colour without documenting probability or impact definitions.","A risk matrix is a decision aid; detailed impact dimensions, thresholds and context remain necessary."],["Quantitative risk analysis and EMV","A decision has a 30 percent chance of a ₦20 million loss and a 70 percent chance of no loss, and management needs a probability-weighted estimate.","Calculate an expected monetary value of a ₦6 million loss while also considering risk appetite and tail exposure.","Record a certain ₦20 million loss because it is the maximum impact.","Record zero because the loss is less likely than no loss.","Average 30 and 20 to obtain a ₦25 million estimate.","EMV multiplies probability by impact: 0.30 × ₦20 million = ₦6 million expected loss, though it does not replace judgement about severe outcomes."],["Threat response strategies","A critical imported component may be delayed by customs, and a qualified local alternative is available at slightly higher cost.","Evaluate avoiding, mitigating, transferring or accepting the threat and select a funded response such as prequalification of the local alternative.","Ignore the threat until the shipment is late.","Record the threat without assigning a response owner.","Assume insurance will automatically prevent schedule delay.","Threat responses should be selected deliberately, assigned, funded and proportionate to probability, impact and risk appetite."],["Opportunity response strategies","A new government data platform could reduce project reporting time by 40 percent if integration is completed early.","Treat the event as an opportunity and consider exploiting, enhancing, sharing or accepting it.","Record only negative uncertainties because opportunities are benefits, not risks.","Reject the platform because it was not known at project initiation.","Delay evaluation until the project has closed.","Risk management includes uncertain events with positive effects; opportunity responses aim to increase probability or benefit."],["Contingency plans and triggers","A risk response says ''use the backup supplier if necessary'' but gives no trigger, owner, lead time or reserved funds.","Define measurable triggers, response ownership, decision timing, required resources and contingency reserve.","Leave the plan vague so the team remains flexible.","Activate the backup supplier immediately for every minor variance.","Record the backup supplier only in meeting minutes.","Actionable contingency plans specify when, by whom and with what resources a response will be activated."],["Issue logging and ownership","A recurring design defect is discussed at meetings but has no formal owner, due date, root-cause action or status.","Record it in the issue log, assign accountable ownership, analyse cause, agree resolution actions and track closure.","Keep discussing it informally until someone volunteers to solve it.","Move it to the risk register and assign no due date.","Close it after the meeting because it has been acknowledged.","Issue management requires structured recording, ownership, resolution, tracking and evidence of closure."],["Issue prioritisation and escalation","A regulatory noncompliance issue exceeds the project manager''s authority, but the team delays escalation to avoid negative attention.","Escalate promptly through the defined governance route with facts, impacts, options and recommended action.","Conceal the issue until a solution is guaranteed.","Wait for the next quarterly report regardless of urgency.","Escalate without evidence and demand an immediate decision.","Issues beyond delegated authority or tolerance should be escalated early with decision-quality information."],["Risk and issue tracking tools","Risk and issue information is duplicated across spreadsheets with conflicting ratings and outdated status.","Establish a controlled source of truth with version control, auditability, ownership and regular reconciliation.","Allow every stakeholder to maintain a separate official register.","Delete historical changes so only the latest rating remains.","Use presentation slides as the master record.","Reliable tracking requires a controlled, current and auditable repository that supports ownership and decision-making."],["Risk communication and transparency","A sponsor receives only a total count of risks and cannot see exposure trends, response effectiveness or decisions needed.","Communicate prioritised exposure, trends, response status, residual risk, emerging issues and required decisions.","Report only the number of closed risks.","Withhold severe risks until they become issues.","Send the full register without interpretation to every stakeholder.","Risk communication should be timely, audience-appropriate and focused on exposure, actions and decisions."],["Risk radar and emerging risk monitoring","The team reviews only known risks and misses weak signals from policy, market, technology and community changes.","Use a risk radar or horizon-scanning process to detect, assess and communicate emerging threats and opportunities.","Freeze the risk register after baseline approval.","Monitor only internal technical risks.","Treat weak signals as irrelevant until impact is certain.","Risk radar and horizon scanning support early awareness of emerging uncertainty before it becomes urgent."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
